# Line endings are LF everywhere, checked out as LF on Windows too.
#
# Not a style preference. Git on Windows defaults to converting to CRLF on checkout, and
# `spec/fixtures/*.json` is a byte-exact contract with the app — the fixture test compares the file's
# bytes against what the encoder produces, so a translated line ending fails the test with two
# visually identical blobs and no explanation. That is exactly what happened on the first CI run.
#
# gofmt also assumes LF, so the same rule keeps Go sources consistent.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The fixtures, explicitly, so this survives someone relaxing the default above. `-text` means Git
# never inspects or converts them: the bytes in the repository are the bytes on disk, which is the
# only useful guarantee for a file whose whole purpose is being compared byte for byte.
spec/fixtures/*.json -text

# Genuinely binary, in case any ever lands here.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.sqlite binary
